// ===========================================================================
// nightshade/tools/settle_shot.hml — LOOK AT THE TOWN            (settlement-render)
// ===========================================================================
//
//   hemlockc -O1 tools/settle_shot.hml -o /tmp/sshot
//   SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy /tmp/sshot --scene list
//   SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy /tmp/sshot --scene settle_city_dusk \
//                                    --out docs/shots/settle_city_dusk.png
//
// 320x180 at g_FOV_HIP, x3 = 960x540.  Resolution and field of view are
// IMPORTED from src/core/config.hml; this file may not name either.  Why that
// sentence is in capitals: see the block above `g_upscale`.
//
// The five contracts `tools/shot.hml` holds itself to, held here too:
//
//  1. It renders through the REAL frame path — `src/render/world_render.hml`,
//     imported, never reimplemented.  `ci_imports.sh` R7 fails the build if any
//     tool defines its own `frame_render`, and this file does not.
//  2. It renders the REAL ART: palette, tod, texgen, skygen, fxgen, meshgen and
//     hubgen.  There is no second art pipeline here.
//  3. Deterministic: no wall clock and no `@stdlib/random` reaches any geometry
//     decision.  Same arguments, same PNG bytes.
//  4. Compiled only.
//  5. A screenshot without its stats report is impossible: the PNG and the
//     `.stats.txt` are written as a pair or neither survives.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THIS EXISTS RATHER THAN A NEW `--scene` IN shot.hml
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `tools/shot.hml` is not this task's file (CLAUDE.md §3, and other agents are
// in this tree right now).  Its scenes also build their terrain from
// `world_render.hml`'s `terrain_h`, which is NOT the heightfield the settlement
// stands on — `settle.hml` places everything on `worldgen_height`.  A
// settlement scene in shot.hml would therefore float.  This harness builds its
// ground from `worldgen_height`, exactly as `src/game/main.hml` does.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW THE SETTLEMENT IS DRIVEN TO A TIER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Not by poking `st.tier`.  A synthetic LANTERN GRID is handed to
// `settle_lanterns_set` and then `settle_step` is run over real in-game days
// until the ladder promotes and the work sites finish — i.e. the picture is of
// a settlement that EARNED its tier through the same code path the game uses.
// If the ladder ever stops working, this tool stops producing a city.
// ===========================================================================

